Consider the lines \begin{align*}
y&=3x+5 \\ 2y&=4x+5 \\ 3y&=9x-2 \\ 2y&=x-3 \\ 4y&=x-5.
\end{align*}Let's say that a pair of lines is $\emph{good}$ if the two lines are either parallel or perpendicular to each other.  Among all the pairs of lines shown, how many pairs are good?

response:
First split the equations into slope-intercept form. The equations in slope-intercept form are: \begin{align*}
y&=3x+5 \\ y&=2x+\dfrac{5}{2} \\ y&=3x-\dfrac{2}{3} \\ y&=\dfrac{1}{2}x-\dfrac{3}{2} \\ y&=\dfrac{1}{4}x-\dfrac{5}{4}.
\end{align*}There are two lines with slope 3, so $\binom{2}{2}=1$ pair with the same slope. There are two lines with slope $\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $\binom{2}{2}=1$ pair with the same slope. There are four lines, so $\binom{4}{2}=6$ total pairs. Two lines are parallel if their slopes are the same, so there are 2 parallel pairs, and two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is $-1$, so there are 2 perpendicular pairs. 2+2+1=5 pairs are good.</think> <answer>5</answer>

